WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:47.208 --> 00:00:52.625
For the next 10 days, it’s just me and 
my camper van with no fixed plans.

00:00:52.916 --> 00:00:57.708
Just a rough route and a lot of time to explore Iceland.

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:21.833
The Kjölur Route (35) is a highland road 
that cuts through Iceland’s remote interior.

00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:28.208
It takes you through rough gravel roads, passing some of Iceland’s most remote scenery along the way.

00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:46.833
I made my way to a glacial lake that has become 
one of my favorite places to stop along the route.

00:02:34.666 --> 00:02:41.208
Hvítárvatn is a glacial lake fed by meltwater 
from the nearby Langjökull glacier.

00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:47.250
The lake feeds into the Hvítá River, 
which eventually flows toward Gullfoss.

00:02:56.791 --> 00:02:59.083
Time for a quick dinner.

00:02:59.916 --> 00:03:03.291
Having a kitchen in the camper gives you freedom. 

00:03:03.791 --> 00:03:09.208
You can stop whenever you want and cook a simple meal, 
and be more self-sufficient on the road.

00:03:09.791 --> 00:03:13.458
Tonight's dinner is Chicken Tikka Masala.

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:27.458
What a beautiful spot to have a meal,
moments like this make me feel incredibly lucky.

00:03:47.541 --> 00:03:54.166
The sun is setting and it's time to make my way 
to a campsite near Kerlingarfjöll.

00:04:25.375 --> 00:04:31.250
Hveradalir is the most visited geothermal area 
in the Kerlingarfjöll highlands.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:37.666
It's known for its steaming vents, bubbling mud pools, 
and colorful rhyolite mountains.

00:04:39.791 --> 00:04:45.083
Ice and snow were beginning to cover the area, 
making it tricky to navigate.

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:56.541
Hveradalir means “valley of hot springs".

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:03.791
There’s also folklore tied to Kerlingarfjöll, which means something like “old woman mountains”.

00:05:04.083 --> 00:05:10.375
According to legend, a troll woman called Kerling, was said to have turned to stone when caught by the morning sun.

00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:30.125
I’d heard about a nearby hot spring, 
so I decided to head over and check it out.

00:06:43.458 --> 00:06:49.416
The water was nice and warm. 
I can't believe I had this all to myself.

00:07:10.416 --> 00:07:13.625
I really didn't want to get out.

00:07:18.791 --> 00:07:24.541
On the road again and heading to a waterfall.

00:07:35.166 --> 00:07:40.583
Háifoss is one of Iceland’s tallest waterfalls, plunging about 120 meters into a rugged canyon. 

00:07:40.916 --> 00:07:45.291
Its name translates to “high waterfall”.

00:07:52.875 --> 00:07:57.250
Up close, you can truly feel the scale of the waterfall.

00:08:54.375 --> 00:08:57.958
I drive along the F208 towards Landmannalaugar.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:03.208
But before reaching Landmannalaugar, 
there are a few interesting sights along the way.

00:09:35.666 --> 00:09:40.041
Going on a little side trip to get a shot of a crater lake.

00:09:45.125 --> 00:09:45.791
Hey, what's up, everyone?

00:10:28.583 --> 00:10:31.750
The glacial rivers are a big part of traveling 
through the Highlands. 

00:10:32.333 --> 00:10:39.625
The water comes from melting glaciers, carrying fine sediment that gives it that milky, almost grey-blue color. 

00:11:21.916 --> 00:11:29.041
Approaching the Landmannalaugar campsite, 
where I plan to camp for the night.

00:11:29.041 --> 00:11:33.250
But first I need to cross two rivers.

00:11:36.625 --> 00:11:41.916
This one doesn’t look too deep, so it should be fairly easy.

00:11:44.541 --> 00:11:51.000
For river crossings, keeping it in first or second gear 
is the way to go. Slow and steady.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.083
This crossing doesn’t look too bad either.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:06.208
If the crossing looked deeper, 
I’d stay closer to the sides and follow the markers.

00:12:06.833 --> 00:12:13.666
I definitely went a little too fast here, so this is one of those “do as I say, not as I do” moments.

00:12:19.041 --> 00:12:23.500
There were already quite a few campers here.

00:12:24.875 --> 00:12:26.083
Alright guys so,

00:12:26.083 --> 00:12:28.708
I've camped here at Landmannalaugar, 

00:12:28.708 --> 00:12:29.833
My voice is getting...

00:12:29.916 --> 00:12:30.916
It's getting a little bit.

00:12:31.083 --> 00:12:31.791
wheezy.

00:12:31.875 --> 00:12:33.500
I'm gonna try and sleep a little bit early

00:12:33.625 --> 00:12:37.500
so I can wake up early, 
and hike around Landmannalaugar,

00:12:37.541 --> 00:12:38.958
before I head out again.

00:12:38.958 --> 00:12:42.125
With that said, I'm all cosied up here, 
in the camper van.

00:12:42.125 --> 00:12:43.375
For now, this is Allan.

00:12:43.583 --> 00:12:44.458
See you in the morning.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:05.833
Landmannalaugar is a geothermal area in the Highlands, 
known for its colorful rhyolite mountains.

00:13:06.666 --> 00:13:13.541
Landmannalaugar is also the starting point 
of the famous Laugavegur Trail, a multi-day trek.

00:13:25.375 --> 00:13:31.041
The colorful mountains here are made of rhyolite, 
a type of volcanic rock rich in minerals.

00:13:31.791 --> 00:13:38.375
Over time, those minerals react with the elements, 
creating shades of red, yellow, green, and blue.

00:14:01.208 --> 00:14:06.958
The weather had taken a turn for the worse, 
but it was time to head out.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:12.500
The river from yesterday looked deeper, 
likely from all the rain.

00:14:23.416 --> 00:14:27.375
I continued south on the F208, which would 
eventually lead me out of the Highlands.

00:14:27.791 --> 00:14:32.250
But before that there's one spot I wanted to visit.

00:14:45.208 --> 00:14:48.458
Langisjór means “long lake".

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:54.875
The lake is fed by springs and clear streams, giving the water a clearer, deeper blue color than many glacial lakes.

00:15:12.583 --> 00:15:17.875
On the F208, there’s one major river crossing 
before we're out of the Highlands.

00:15:18.416 --> 00:15:27.041
The river doesn’t look too bad today, but if you’re unsure, it’s always worth walking across first to check the depth.

00:15:53.291 --> 00:15:58.125
I made my way towards the village of Vík, 
where I’d camp for the night.

00:16:11.125 --> 00:16:16.666
I decided to visit some of the major sights 
along Iceland’s southern coast.

00:16:52.875 --> 00:16:55.750
It was nice to have a proper meal.

00:17:28.333 --> 00:17:32.083
So my voice is recovering a bit.

00:17:32.416 --> 00:17:37.625
But again, I've just been chugging warm,
hot tea any chance I get.

00:17:37.916 --> 00:17:41.541
I just found this sea stack or rock kind
of in the middle of nowhere.

00:17:41.791 --> 00:17:46.375
And because it's only surrounded by
the black sort of sandy beach,

00:17:46.666 --> 00:17:51.666
it makes it stand out a lot more than it
was placed any other place, I think.

00:17:51.875 --> 00:17:56.541
That obviously makes it a much more interesting
subject for photos or video.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:02.041
So I went ahead and hiked all the way out
there just to get a few shots

00:18:02.041 --> 00:18:04.583
with the camera and the drone.

00:18:05.041 --> 00:18:06.708
Other than it being a bit cold and a bit

00:18:06.708 --> 00:18:08.875
windy, the environment here
is just gorgeous.

00:18:09.875 --> 00:18:12.500
I'm going to go to my camper van, and we're
going to find a campsite for the night

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:14.041
before our next adventure.

00:18:43.375 --> 00:18:50.458
I decided on a 16.5 km loop hike, 
combining parts of the Mælifell and Austurafréttur trails.

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:55.458
I recommend doing the hike counterclockwise 
for better views of the glacier.

00:18:56.458 --> 00:19:01.166
After the first ascent, the trail is pretty much flat 
all the way to the glacier.

00:19:02.791 --> 00:19:07.541
The loop I did takes between 4 to 5 hours to complete.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:15.083
It takes you through some of Þakgil’s most dramatic scenery, including moss-covered canyons and glacier views.

00:19:15.666 --> 00:19:22.208
The area still feels a bit wild and untouched compared to the main sights along the southern coast.

00:20:57.708 --> 00:21:01.750
I head back to the campsite to make a quick lunch.

00:21:05.458 --> 00:21:08.791
Today’s lunch is as simple as it gets: sausages and eggs.

00:21:35.208 --> 00:21:35.875
Oh, it's good.

00:21:36.208 --> 00:21:41.708
So, Þakgil, highly recommend you swinging
by if you got a camper van.

00:21:42.125 --> 00:21:43.250
You want to do some hiking,

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:44.583
spend an entire day here.

00:21:44.833 --> 00:21:45.791
It's so worth it.

00:21:45.958 --> 00:21:47.375
It's just such a beautiful place.

00:21:47.666 --> 00:21:48.583
The campsite is great.

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:51.000
One of my favorite places in
Iceland, definitely.

00:21:53.041 --> 00:21:54.375
I do have a question for you guys.

00:21:54.416 --> 00:21:57.541
Do you just slice the yolk
and eat it like that?

00:21:57.958 --> 00:22:03.375
Or do you like slice all the egg whites and then
you scoop up the egg yolk intact?

00:22:03.625 --> 00:22:04.500
Which way do you do it?

00:22:04.541 --> 00:22:08.458
There's only one right way, in my opinion,
which is you cut like the egg whites and

00:22:08.458 --> 00:22:10.500
then you keep the yolk and then
you scoop it up, right?

00:22:10.916 --> 00:22:12.166
There's only one right way, guys.

00:22:12.333 --> 00:22:15.041
If you disagree, let me know
in the comments below.

00:22:15.166 --> 00:22:17.750
I really do want to know if anyone
does that differently.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:22.625
Apparently, Lisa, my partner, did it the
other way before she met me.

00:22:22.833 --> 00:22:27.750
And I could not accept such a transgression
on the holiness that is the egg yolk.

00:22:28.125 --> 00:22:28.916
I think we're good.

00:22:28.916 --> 00:22:29.666
I'm going to wash up.

00:22:29.875 --> 00:22:30.541
I'll see you guys.

00:24:11.625 --> 00:24:16.000
The landscape around Laki feels completely otherworldly. 

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:22.083
The green moss covers lava fields formed, 
during the Laki eruption of 1783 to 1784.

00:24:22.083 --> 00:24:29.500
The eruption poisoned farmland and livestock, leading to famine and the death of 20% of Iceland’s population.

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:36.916
It’s strange to think that a place this beautiful was shaped by something as catastrophic as the Laki eruption.

00:25:15.541 --> 00:25:21.916
No solo road trip is complete 
without some good music along the way.

00:25:37.958 --> 00:25:45.583
Jökulsárlón is one of Iceland’s most famous glacier lagoons, where huge icebergs slowly drift toward the Atlantic.

00:26:06.916 --> 00:26:09.583
Spotted a seal.

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:18.541
I made my way to a campsite near Vestrahorn for the night.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:09.583
I decided to hike to a lesser known 
waterfall called Strútsfoss.

00:28:10.416 --> 00:28:14.375
It’s a two-tiered waterfall, listed 
at around 120 meters in height.

00:28:15.083 --> 00:28:21.916
It's one of the tallest waterfalls in Iceland, but it feels much quieter and less visited than the nearby Hengifoss.

00:29:12.166 --> 00:29:18.291
That evening, I decided to grab dinner 
at a restaurant in Seyðisfjörður.

00:29:23.416 --> 00:29:29.000
The lamb chops were tender, flavorful, 
and exactly what I needed after a hike.

00:29:39.708 --> 00:29:48.000
I decided to hang out in Seydisfjordur for a bit, wandering around the small town and taking in the atmosphere.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:41.083
I made a stop at Stuðlagil Canyon, 
one of the more unique places in East Iceland.

00:30:41.625 --> 00:30:47.333
The canyon is known for its basalt columns and the blue-green river running through it,

00:31:01.583 --> 00:31:06.250
As you can see, it was pretty busy here, so if you can,
I’d recommend visiting early in the morning.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:31.416
Dettifoss is one of the most powerful waterfalls in Europe.

00:31:32.083 --> 00:31:36.916
when up close you can really feel the force of the water 
as it crashes into the canyon below.

00:31:48.875 --> 00:31:53.666
Hverir is an active geothermal area near Lake Mývatn. 

00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:01.125
The whole place is filled with bubbling mud pots, steaming vents, and a strong smell of sulphur in the air.

00:32:27.041 --> 00:32:31.000
Grafarkirkja is one of Iceland’s oldest turf churches.

00:32:31.541 --> 00:32:39.416
The church is surrounded by a circular turf wall, with parts of the structure dating back to the 17th century.

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:20.416
I found this abandoned barn on the side of the road.

00:34:10.125 --> 00:34:15.958
One of the best parts of traveling around Iceland 
is getting to visit natural hot springs.

00:34:16.583 --> 00:34:22.125
Some of my favorite moments from this trip 
have been soaking in natural hot springs like this.

00:34:22.541 --> 00:34:28.083
I much prefer these smaller, more remote hot springs over the more commercialized ones, like the Blue Lagoon.

00:34:37.833 --> 00:34:38.458
I'm all red.

00:34:38.708 --> 00:34:39.166
What the hell?

00:34:39.166 --> 00:34:40.416
I'm all red, probably from all the heat.

00:34:41.833 --> 00:34:43.083
Anyways, time to leave.

00:34:43.208 --> 00:34:44.375
Thank you for the dip, Iceland.

00:34:44.416 --> 00:34:45.125
Thank you for the dip.

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:57.625
Begin the day with some breakfast.

00:34:58.083 --> 00:35:03.208
After being on the road for a while, you start to realize how little you actually need in life. 

00:35:03.541 --> 00:35:08.541
A warm meal, a good night’s sleep, 
and suddenly everything feels alright.

00:35:08.750 --> 00:35:16.500
This is why I love these kinds of trips. 
It allows me to hit reset and disconnect from modern life.

00:35:48.166 --> 00:35:55.916
Rauðisandur means “red sand,” and it’s very different from the volcanic black sand beaches Iceland is known for.

00:35:56.208 --> 00:36:04.125
Unlike black sand beaches, Rauðisandur gets its color from crushed seashells and coastal sediments. 

00:36:38.416 --> 00:36:42.875
I drove down a dirt road in search of a secluded, lesser-known natural hot spring.

00:36:42.875 --> 00:36:46.916
I ended up staying for about an hour 
and had the whole place to myself.

00:36:47.665 --> 00:36:51.665
The hot spring had a smaller pool and a larger one.

00:36:54.875 --> 00:36:57.583
Time to test the bigger pool.

00:36:59.583 --> 00:37:02.291
It was definitely warmer, but still comfortable.

00:37:03.125 --> 00:37:07.750
This was such an awesome little find, 
and I’m really glad I made the effort to get here.

00:37:08.291 --> 00:37:11.625
It was the perfect end to my time in Iceland.

